    <string name="paper_rules_title">How a paper order fills</string>
    <string name="paper_rules_intro">Nothing here fills at the mid price. Every number you see comes from one of these rules.</string>
    <string name="paper_rule_spread">A buy pays the ask and a sell hits the bid. Where the feed sends no book, an assumed spread of %1$s%% is widened around last and the fill says it was assumed.</string>
    <string name="paper_rule_slippage">Market orders and triggered stops also pay %1$s%% slippage. An order that rested never slips.</string>
    <string name="paper_rule_resting">A resting order fills at its own price and pays the maker fee; one that takes the market pays the taker fee.</string>
    <string name="paper_rule_stop">A stop is a trigger, not a price. When it goes it fills wherever the market is, not at the number you wrote — and that is what costs beginners the most.</string>
    <string name="paper_rule_unwatched">While the app is shut nobody watches the price cross. Then the worse of the two candidate prices is used: a limit gets its own limit, a stop gets the market — and the fill says it was unwatched.</string>
    <string name="paper_rule_stale">Nothing fills from a price the feed itself calls stale.</string>
    <string name="paper_rule_touch">Here a resting order fills on touch. A real one joins a queue and may watch the price arrive and leave without it. This is optimistic.</string>
    <string name="paper_rule_gap">The app sees a last price, not a candle. A stop that only a wick reached is not hit between two observed prices.</string>
    <string name="paper_rule_execution">This app\'s own one-tap execution sends market orders only. Limits and stops are placed at the exchange or in MetaTrader, so practise them here and enter them there.</string>

